  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On June 26, the IRGC launched at least four one-way attack drones at commercial vessels transiting the Strait of Hormuz, with one striking the Singapore-flagged cargo ship Ever Lovely and damaging its upper deck. The vessel remained operational and proceeded onward, but the attack constituted a direct violation of the June 17 Islamabad MOU — the first formal US-Iran war-termination framework of the 2026 conflict. Trump characterized the attack as a "foolish violation"; CENTCOM struck Iranian missile and drone storage facilities and coastal radar sites within hours. Iran's IRGC issued a counter-retaliation statement and characterized its original drone action as "ceasefire management" under Clause 5 of the MOU, asserting sovereign authority over Hormuz transit arrangements. As of June 27, neither side has formally declared the MOU void, but the framework is under acute stress. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, supports <span class="cite">F5</span> without move</p>

      <p>The June 22-23 Switzerland talks between US and Iranian negotiators produced a jointly acknowledged "roadmap" toward a final deal within 60 days, with three working groups established on oversight, sanctions, and nuclear matters. Qatar and Pakistan issued mediator readouts confirming face-to-face sessions at the Bürgenstock resort. Iran's Foreign Ministry described the technical talks as concluding "successfully." The Islamabad MOU, signed remotely by both presidents on June 17, had already established a 60-day toll-free Hormuz reopening clause and a $12 billion frozen-asset release arrangement, along with a 60-day sanctions waiver from the US Treasury. These events confirm that both <span class="cite">F1</span> and <span class="cite">F2</span> resolve — formal convened sessions between named principals occurred, and Iran's June 1 suspension was functionally rescinded through its active participation. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>The dispute over IAEA access to Iran's nuclear facilities widened in the June 24-27 window. IAEA Director General Rafael Grossi stated on June 26 that the MOU grants inspectors access to Iranian nuclear facilities and that visits "will happen." Iran's deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi contradicted this on June 25, stating there are "no plans" for inspector access before a final deal. Iran's MFA spokesman Baghaei separately confirmed no schedule for IAEA visits. The June 26 US-Iran military exchange further complicated any near-term inspection arrangement, with Iran now in a posture of controlled defiance on the nuclear file even as it nominally maintains the ceasefire framework. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Knesset dissolution proceedings remain between the first and final readings. The dissolution bill passed its first plenum reading 106-0 in early June, with the House Committee having advanced it 9-0 on June 1. Coalition whip Ofir Katz explicitly deferred the insertion of a fixed election date to the period before the final two readings, leaving the bill in procedural suspension. Netanyahu favors an October election date while Shas and UTJ prefer September. No second or third reading has been scheduled or conducted in the June 10-27 window. The Iran ceasefire management crisis introduced by the June 26 Hormuz incident gives Netanyahu additional political reasons to delay domestic calendar finalization, as a visible external security crisis typically benefits an incumbent wartime PM. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>CENTCOM confirmed on June 16 that US forces commenced mine-countermeasure operations in the Strait of Hormuz — the day after the MOU framework was virtually signed. The UK's RFA Lyme Bay arrived in the Middle East to join a multinational force comprising France, UK, Germany, Italy, Japan, and Canada. Al Jazeera published a detailed visual explainer of the ongoing minesweeping methodology on June 25. Estimates of the mine inventory center around 80 Maham 3 and Maham 7 blast mines. The June 26 IRGC drone strike and subsequent US-Iran military exchange introduced acute risk to MCM mission continuity, but as of June 27 no authoritative source has reported a pause or termination of clearing operations. The commencement criterion for <span class="cite">F5</span> is already met; the residual question is whether operations remain active through July 10. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

