      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> The June 26 IRGC drone strike and US counter-strike, combined with Iran's assertion of sovereign Hormuz authority under Clause 5, document a tit-for-tat pattern that could collapse <span style="color:var(--accent);font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F8</span> (ceasefire-holds, p=0.52) and drive Hormuz transits toward zero; <span style="color:var(--accent);font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F4</span> effectively eliminated, <span style="color:var(--accent);font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F10</span> unlikely.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Neither side formally voids the MOU but the 60-day roadmap stalls on IAEA access (<span style="color:var(--accent);font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F9</span> blocked, p=0.30) and Hormuz reopening (<span style="color:var(--accent);font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F10</span> unlikely, p=0.14), producing a frozen partial-ceasefire with sporadic incidents and no durable shipping recovery through July.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> Mediators (Qatar/Pakistan) stabilize the MOU channel, <span style="color:var(--accent);font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F8</span> resolves YES, a Grossi Tehran visit is announced (<span style="color:var(--accent);font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F9</span> upward), and the MCM mission (<span style="color:var(--accent);font-family:monospace;font-size:0.9em;">F5</span>, p=0.78) produces clearing progress that begins to lift commercial insurer confidence above the 15-transit-per-day threshold.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>State media outlets IRNA, Tasnim, and Press TV framed the June 26 drone strike on the Ever Lovely as a legitimate act of "ceasefire management" rather than a violation of the Islamabad MOU. Parliamentary security commission chief Ebrahim Azizi published a statement asserting that the Strait of Hormuz "is governed by Iran" and that Washington's characterization of the action as a ceasefire breach was rejected. The IRGC official communiqué cited Clause 5 of the MOU as conferring on Iran sole authority over transit arrangements in the waterway. State media carried no acknowledgment that the US CENTCOM strikes caused casualties or material damage, describing the US response as "limited" and warning that "if aggression is repeated, the response will be broader." → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>On the nuclear file, Iranian state media gave prominent coverage to the Foreign Ministry's rebuttal of VP Vance's June 23 claim that Iran had agreed to IAEA inspectors. Spokesman Baghaei's statement that there is "no clear schedule" for IAEA access and that Iran has not met with DG Grossi was framed as a defense of sovereignty and a corrective to what state media described as US "misrepresentation" of the MOU text. Deputy FM Gharibabadi's June 25 statement — that access would come only after a final deal and only after sanctions relief — was presented as the definitive Iranian position. Iranian commentary described IAEA DG Grossi's June 26 assertion that inspections "will happen" as a unilateral interpretation that does not bind Tehran. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>The Switzerland technical talks of June 21-23 received measured coverage in Iranian state media: IRNA described the sessions as concluding "successfully" and cited the establishment of three working groups as a sign of Iranian negotiating seriousness. State media did not prominently feature the $12 billion frozen-asset arrangement or the IAEA access dispute in the same coverage — compartmentalizing the economic gains from the nuclear sovereignty question. The Islamabad MOU was framed as a framework Iran entered on its own terms, with state media noting the 60-day window as a concession of process rather than principle. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

